Let $\Theta\in\widehat C$ be the actual quasi-$R$ intertwiner of [[thm-the-weightwise-quasi-r-intertwiner-exists-and-is-unique]] and let the based modules be the exact four-axiom based modules of this page with divided-power lattices. Then integral **action** is proved in the following cases:

(a) $V(\lambda)\otimes N$ for any based $N$, with the highest weight module first;

(b) $M\otimes\omega V(\lambda)$ for any based $M$, with the lowest module second, under vectorwise finite action;

(c) arbitrary $U_A$-stable based pairs in finite Cartan type;

(d) a first factor in the class $C_{\mathrm{high}}$ with arbitrary based second, or an arbitrary based first with second in $C_{\mathrm{low}}$, under vectorwise finite action, where $C_{\mathrm{high}}$ consists of exact based subquotients of weight-admissible direct sums of finite highest tensor constructions and $C_{\mathrm{low}}$ is its lowest or unsigned-Chevalley counterpart, based subobjects being saturated basis subsets and quotient maps being integral and bar compatible. The classes $C_{\mathrm{high}},C_{\mathrm{low}}$ are closed under the normalized finite tensor construction.

In particular all finite highest products and all ordered highest-prefix, arbitrary-based-middle, lowest-suffix products have the integral tensor action and construction; direct sums require the stated upper-cone or finite-component conditions, and in the lowest case the explicit lower-cone action bound is used. No arbitrary based $O_{\mathrm{int}}$ pair is covered in general type.

In these cases $\Theta$ preserves the actual integral tensor lattice and has $A$-valued action coefficients in its tensor bases; its degree-$0$ component is the identity; every positive root component shifts the first label up and the second down, so the bar is locally weight-transfer triangular with diagonal $1$. In finite Cartan type the stronger algebra coefficients $\Theta_\nu\in U_A^+[\nu]\otimes U_A^-[-\nu]$ are also proved, and that stronger universal coefficient claim is false in general affine type. A normalized triangular bar-correction constructs the genuine tensor global basis, so raw tensor products of lower basis vectors need not themselves be bar fixed. The class in (d) means based subquotients of the constructed tensor bases, not arbitrary integral lattices in semisimple field modules, and the affine construction below disproves unqualified arbitrary-based action integrality while retaining the stated general symmetrizable cases.

Let $A_0$ be the local ring of [[def-the-local-ring-at-q-zero]] and let $M$ be an integrable weight module with its Kashiwara operators $\tilde e_i,\tilde f_i$ ([[def-kashiwara-crystal-operators]], [[def-quantum-weight-module-and-integrability]]).

A **crystal lattice** of $M$ is a free $A_0$-submodule $L\subseteq M$ such that

(i) $L$ generates $M$ over $\mathbb Q(q)$, i.e. the canonical map $\mathbb Q(q)\otimes_{A_0}L\to M$ is an isomorphism;

(ii) $L=\bigoplus_{\mu\in P}L_\mu$ with $L_\mu:=L\cap M_\mu$, and each $L_\mu$ is again a free $A_0$-module;

(iii) $\tilde e_iL\subseteq L$ and $\tilde f_iL\subseteq L$ for all $i\in I$.

Because $A_0$ is a principal ideal domain, each graded piece $L_\mu$ has a well-defined rank $\operatorname{rank}_{A_0}L_\mu$, and (ii) says that $\operatorname{rank}_{A_0}L$ is the sum of the ranks of the $L_\mu$.

A **crystal basis** of $M$ is a pair $(L,B)$ consisting of a crystal lattice $L$ and a $\mathbb Q$-basis $B$ of the crystal limit $L/qL\cong\mathbb Q\otimes_{A_0}L$ ([[def-the-local-ring-at-q-zero]]) such that

(iv) $B=\bigsqcup_{\mu\in P}B_\mu$, where $B_\mu:=B\cap(L_\mu/qL_\mu)$;

(v) $\tilde e_iB\subseteq B\cup\{0\}$ and $\tilde f_iB\subseteq B\cup\{0\}$ for all $i\in I$;

(vi) for all $b,b'\in B$ and all $i$: $\tilde f_ib=b'$ if and only if $b=\tilde e_ib'$; here $\tilde e_i,\tilde f_i$ denote the operators induced on $L/qL$.

The **crystal graph** of $(L,B)$ is the coloured directed graph with vertex set $B$ and an arrow $b\xrightarrow{i}b'$ whenever $\tilde f_ib=b'$ (by (vi) this is equivalent to $b=\tilde e_ib'$). The **crystal of $M$** is this graph, together with the weight function $b\mapsto\mu$ for $b\in B_\mu$. For every $\mu\in P$ one has $\dim_{\mathbb Q(q)}M_\mu=\operatorname{rank}_{A_0}L_\mu=\#B_\mu$.

A **crystal basis of the negative half** $U_q^-$ is defined by the same data with $U_q^-$ in place of $M$: a free $A_0$-submodule $L\subseteq U_q^-$ with $U_q^-\cong\mathbb Q(q)\otimes_{A_0}L$ and $\tilde e_iL,\tilde f_iL\subseteq L$ ([[thm-the-i-string-decomposition-of-the-negative-half]]), together with a $\mathbb Q$-basis $B$ of $L/qL$ satisfying (v) and (vi) for the operators on the negative half. Here the root grading replaces the weight grading, and the lattice and residue basis are required to respect that root grading. In the negative half, $\tilde e_i\tilde f_i=\mathrm{id}$ implies $\tilde f_iB\subseteq B$; this refinement is specific to its infinite strings. No choice principle is used in these definitions.

Let $(I,A,D,P,P^{\vee},q)$ be a symmetrizable Cartan datum ([[def-symmetrizable-cartan-datum-for-a-quantum-group]], [[def-generalized-cartan-matrix]]), let $M$ be a chosen $P$-graded type-1 quantum weight module with $E_i$ and $F_i$ locally nilpotent ([[def-quantum-weight-module-and-integrability]]), and fix $i\in I$. For $\mu\in P$ put
$$H_\mu:=\ker(E_i)\cap M_\mu,\qquad a_\mu:=\langle\mu,h_i\rangle .$$
Then:

(i) $H_\mu=0$ whenever $a_\mu<0$;

(ii) if $a_\mu\ge0$, then $F_i^{(n)}:H_\mu\to M_{\mu-n\alpha_i}$ is injective for $0\le n\le a_\mu$ and is the zero map for $n>a_\mu$;

(iii) every $u\in M_\lambda$ has a unique finite **admissible decomposition** $u=\sum_{n\ge0}F_i^{(n)}u_n$ with $u_n\in H_{\lambda+n\alpha_i}$ and $u_n=0$ whenever $n>\langle\lambda+n\alpha_i,h_i\rangle$;

(iv) consequently $M_\lambda$ is the internal direct sum of the nonzero admissible string spaces $F_i^{(n)}H_{\lambda+n\alpha_i}$.

Coefficients beyond a string's bottom are specified to be zero; arbitrary killed coefficients at the top are not allowed. No finite-dimensionality of $M$ or of its weight spaces, no bounded support, no uniform nilpotence and no choice principle is assumed; $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$ is the integer pairing of the datum ([[def-kac-moody-integral-and-dominant-integral-weights]]) and $F_i^{(n)}$ is the divided power of [[def-quantum-integers-factorials-and-divided-powers-at-q-i]].

